# --- WordPress archive surfaces: DO NOT redirect these -----------------------
# /category/*, /tag/*, /feed/ and paginated /page/<n>/ are crawl artefacts from the
# old software: listings generated from content that has already moved, never pages
# anybody wrote. Google still has them indexed.
#
# They are not redirected, for the reason this file refuses every other soft-404
# shape: an archive of posts that moved has no single successor, and dumping the
# whole surface onto /codes/ helps nobody.
#
# They are no longer 410 either. Each prefix used to be a catch-all route returning
# 410, which meant a metered Function invocation for every request to an unbounded
# set of paths — /page/1/ through /page/9999/ are all valid, and one crawler walking
# them could spend a fifth of the daily free-plan budget on listings nobody wrote.
# They now fall through to the static 404.html, which costs nothing to serve. The
# trade is named in full at `retiredArchivePrefixes` in `src/data/gone.ts`.
#
# A named 301 for one path beneath a prefix still works and still wins, because
# first match wins here. That is the intended escape hatch when an archive path
# does turn out to have a real successor.
#
# `test/gone.test.mjs` asserts no catch-all comes back under these prefixes without
# that decision being revisited, and that none is redirected wholesale.

